<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bakhtiyar Khalji</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1206">1206</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Muhammad Bakhtiyar Khalji</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bakhtiyar Khalji was a Turkic-Afghan commander of the Ghurids who conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> at the end of the 12th century. His raids in Bihar destroyed Buddhist monasteries such as Odantapuri, and around 1204 he surprised the Sena king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmana-sena">Lakshmana Sena</a> at Nadia. He made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Lakhnauti</a> his capital, founding the first Muslim regime in Bengal, but died in 1206 after a disastrous expedition towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bakhtiyar-khalji.jpg" alt="Bakhtiyar Khalji" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Margaret Dovaston · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_end_of_Buddhist_Monks,_A.D._1193_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Muslim rule in Bengal began with the conquests of <b>Bakhtiyar Khalji</b> around 1204, and for more than a century the province was governed from Lakhnauti by governors of the Delhi Sultanate who often rebelled against their distant overlords.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Mu'izz al-Din relied on Turkic slave generals whom he had bought and trained, such as Qutb al-Din Aybak at Delhi and Lahore, Taj al-Din Yildiz at Kirman and Ghazni, and Nasir al-Din Qabacha at Multan and Uch, together with free commanders such as <b>Muhammad Bakhtiyar Khalji</b> in Bihar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Within a few decades the Turkish campaigns of <b>Bakhtiyar Khalji</b> destroyed the great monasteries of Bihar, including Nalanda and Vikramashila.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pala-empire">Pala Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>It arose in the early 13th century in eastern Bengal, around Vikrampur and the later Suvarnagrama (Sonargaon), as Sena power collapsed in western Bengal after <b>Bakhtiyar Khalji's</b> conquest of Nadia.</blockquote><a href="/state/deva-dynasty-of-bengal">Deva dynasty of Bengal</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Muhammad Bakhtiyar Khalji</b>, a Turkic-Afghan commander serving the Ghurids, raided Bihar and then, around 1204, surprised the Sena king Lakshmana Sena at Nadia.</blockquote><a href="/state/khalji-dynasty-of-bengal">Khalji dynasty of Bengal</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1204 <b>Bakhtiyar Khalji</b> raided Nabadwip, and the aged Lakshmanasena fled east.</blockquote><a href="/state/sena-dynasty">Sena dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Garmsir, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Died in Bangarh, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.12,64.2&amp;year=1196" aria-label="Garmsir on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6209 1030 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Bakhtiyar Khalji"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6659 1300h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6659 1300h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.12,64.2&amp;year=1196">What was here in 1196? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
